### TL;DR

* **Layer type:** `color-relief`  
* **Key paint property:** `color-relief-color` with an `interpolate` expression over `["elevation"]`.  
* **Result:** A client‑side hypsometric tint (blue → green → brown → white) that updates automatically as you pan/zoom, works together with hillshade or 3‑D terrain, and requires no extra raster tiles.

### Short answer  
Yes – you can generate contour lines **on‑the‑fly** from a `raster-dem` source with the **`maplibre‑contour`** library.  
The library runs a Web‑Worker that reads the DEM tiles, extracts elevation isolines, and serves them to MapLibre through a custom protocol URL. You then add a normal `vector` source + a `line` layer (or a `symbol` layer for labels) that points at the generated contour tiles.

Below is a step‑by‑step guide, plus a few gotchas and performance tips.

---

## 1. Install the library

```bash
# npm / yarn / pnpm
npm install maplibre-contour   # or yarn add maplibre-contour
```

> The package works with MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.0 (the current 5.x series). It bundles a small Web‑Worker, so you don’t need to ship any extra worker files yourself.

---

## 2. Create a `DemSource` that knows about your DEM tiles

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';

// 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style you like)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 45,               // optional – just to see the terrain
  antialias: true
});

// 2️⃣  Define the DEM source that will also produce contours
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // URL pattern for the DEM tiles (must be a raster‑dem source)
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
  // How the DEM values are encoded – match the source definition!
  // Common values: 'terrarium', 'mapbox', 'raw' (see the style spec)
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // Highest zoom you expect to request (helps the worker allocate buffers)
  maxzoom: 12,
  // Whether the worker should be spawned (true = default)
  worker: true
});
```

### Why the `encoding` matters
`raster-dem` tiles can be stored as:

| Encoding | How the height is packed |
|----------|--------------------------|
| `mapbox` | 16‑bit signed integer (meters) |
| `terrarium` | 8‑bit RGB → `(R*256 + G + B/256) - 32768` |
| `raw` | 32‑bit float (meters) |

If you get this wrong the contours will be wildly off (or flat). Use the same value you declared in the `raster-dem` source of your style.

---

## 3. Register the source with MapLibre

```js
// This injects the custom protocol handler (mlcontour://) into the map
demSource.setupMaplibre(map);
```

After this call the map can request URLs like `mlcontour://…` and the worker will return a vector tile containing the generated contours.

---

## 4. Add a *virtual* vector source that points at the contour protocol

```js
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The protocol URL is built for each request; you can pass options:
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // ── Contour spacing (in metres) per zoom level
      thresholds: {
        // zoom : [minor, major] intervals
        11: [200, 1000],
        12: [100, 500],
        14: [50, 200]
      },
      // Property names that will appear on each feature
      elevationKey: 'ele',   // numeric elevation of the line (m)
      levelKey: 'level',     // 1 = major (label‑worthy), 0 = minor
      // Optional: give the source a stable name for caching
      contourLayer: 'contours'
    })
  ],
  // The worker can generate tiles up to this zoom; keep it reasonable
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

### What the options do

| Option | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `thresholds` | A map of zoom → `[minor, major]` spacing. The worker will emit two sets of lines: *minor* (thin) and *major* (thicker, labelled). |
| `elevationKey` | Name of the numeric property that holds the line’s elevation (used for styling or labeling). |
| `levelKey` | Name of the integer flag (`0` = minor, `1` = major). Useful for conditional styling (`match` expression). |
| `contourLayer` | Arbitrary string that becomes the *source‑layer* name inside the generated vector tiles. You’ll reference this in `source-layer` when adding a layer. |

---

## 5. Add a line layer (and optionally a label layer)

```js
// 5️⃣  Contour lines
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',   // matches contourLayer above
  paint: {
    // Dark thin lines for minor contours, brighter/thicker for majors
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],   // 1 = major, 0 = minor
      1, 1.5,              // major → 1.5px
      0, 0.8,              // minor → 0.8px
      0.5                  // fallback
    ]
  }
});

// 6️⃣  Optional: labels for major contours
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],   // only major lines
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
    'text-size': 10,
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

That’s it – the contours will appear as soon as the map requests the DEM tiles for the current view. The worker runs in the background, so the UI stays responsive.

---

## 6. Full minimal example (HTML)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre runtime contours</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
import mlcontour from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/maplibre-contour@1.2.0/+esm';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // The DEM source that will also be used for terrain (optional)
      'terrain-dem': {
        type: 'raster-dem',
        url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
        tileSize: 256,
        encoding: 'terrarium'
      },
      // A simple raster basemap (replace with your own)
      'sat': {
        type: 'raster',
        url: 'https://tiles.mapbox.com/v4/mapbox.satellite/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg?access_token=YOUR_TOKEN',
        tileSize: 256
      }
    },
    layers: [
      { id: 'sat', type: 'raster', source: 'sat' }
    ]
  },
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 45,
  antialias: true
});

// ---- DEM source that also produces contours ----
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  maxzoom: 12,
  worker: true
});
demSource.setupMaplibre(map);

// ---- Virtual vector source for contours ----
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      thresholds: {11: [200,1000], 12: [100,500], 14: [50,200]},
      elevationKey: 'ele',
      levelKey: 'level',
      contourLayer: 'contours'
    })
  ],
  maxzoom: 15
});

// ---- Contour line layer ----
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  paint: {
    'line-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.6)',
    'line-width': [
      'match', ['get', 'level'],
      1, 1.5,
      0, 0.8,
      0.5
    ]
  }
});

// ---- Optional label layer ----
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
    'text-size': 10,
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page, pan/zoom, and you’ll see contour lines appear instantly as the DEM tiles load.

---

## 7. Performance & practical tips

| Issue | Recommendation |
|-------|----------------|
| **Worker startup cost** | The first request will spin up the Web‑Worker and decode the first DEM tile – expect a ~200 ms pause on low‑end devices. Subsequent tiles are fast. |
| **Too many zoom levels** | Keep `maxzoom` on the `DemSource` modest (12‑13). The worker builds a mesh per tile; higher zoom → more tiles → more CPU. |
| **Contour density** | Very fine thresholds (e.g., 5 m spacing) can generate thousands of line segments per tile and stall the UI. Start with 50‑200 m and adjust. |
| **Styling major vs. minor** | Use the `level` property (`0` = minor, `1` = major) in a `match` expression for width/color, and filter the label layer on `level === 1`. |
| **Caching** | The worker caches decoded DEM tiles in memory. If you have a long‑running app, consider calling `demSource.clearCache()` when you know the map will stay on a very different region for a while. |
| **Multiple DEM sources** | Only one `DemSource` can be active per map at a time (the protocol is global). If you need two different DEMs, you must create two separate `Map` instances or merge the DEMs server‑side. |
| **Terrain + contours** | You can enable 3‑D terrain (`map.setTerrain({source: 'terrain-dem'})`) *and* draw contours on top – they’ll drape correctly because both read the same DEM. |
| **Browser support** | The library uses `OffscreenCanvas` when available (Chrome, Edge, Firefox). In older browsers it falls back to a normal `Worker` + `ImageBitmap`. No extra polyfills needed. |

---

## 8. Common pitfalls & how to debug them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No contours appear** | `demSource.setupMaplibre(map)` not called, or the custom protocol URL is malformed. | Open the browser dev‑tools → Network tab. Look for requests to `mlcontour://…`. If none, the setup step failed. |
| **Contours are flat (all at 0 m)** | Wrong `encoding` (e.g., using `terrarium` while the DEM is `mapbox`). | Verify the `encoding` field in the `raster-dem` source of your style JSON. Use the same string here. |
| **Contours look jagged / missing at high zoom** | `maxzoom` on the `DemSource` is lower than the map’s current zoom, so the worker falls back to lower‑resolution tiles. | Raise `maxzoom` on the `DemSource` (and ensure the DEM tiles exist at that zoom). |
| **

Below is a **step‑by‑step recipe** that turns the empty “page‑background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All of the pieces are part of the **core MapLibre GL JS API** – no extra plugins are needed.

---

## 1️⃣  Why the background is showing

| Symptom | What’s really happening |
|---------|------------------------|
| The map looks fine on the ground, but everything above the horizon is a solid colour (or the page’s CSS background) | The style either (a) has a **background layer** that is opaque, or (b) does **not have a `sky` object** defined. In MapLibre the sky is **not a layer** – it lives at the root of the style (or is added with `map.setSky()`). If it is missing, the renderer falls back to the page’s background colour. |

So we have to **(i) add a sky object** and **(ii) make sure no opaque background layer blocks it**.

---

## 2️⃣  Add a sky object (or call `map.setSky()`)

You can do it **once** when you create the map, **or** later with `map.setSky()`.  
Both ways are equivalent – the API just writes the same `sky` object into the style.

### 2.1  In the style JSON (static way)

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "terrain-dem": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "url": "https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json",
      "tileSize": 256,
      "maxzoom": 12
    }
    /* …other sources… */
  },

  "terrain": {
    "source": "terrain-dem",
    "exaggeration": 1
  },

  "sky": {
    "sky-color": "#80c0ff",          // light blue of the upper sky
    "sky-horizon-blend": 0.5,        // how quickly the colour fades toward the horizon
    "horizon-color": "#ffffff",     // colour of the line where sky meets ground
    "horizon-fog-blend": 0.5,        // blend between horizon colour and fog colour
    "fog-color": "#c0d8ff",          // colour of the haze that meets the ground
    "fog-ground-blend": 0.5,         // how far the fog reaches down the terrain
    "atmosphere-blend": 0.5          // optional – adds a subtle atmospheric glow
  },

  "layers": [
    /* …your hillshade / imagery / vector layers… */
  ]
}
```

> **Tip:** The sky object is **root‑level**, i.e. a sibling of `sources`, `layers`, `terrain`, etc. It is **not** a layer with `type: "sky"` (MapLibre never had that).

### 2.2  Dynamically with `map.setSky()` (if you already have a style loaded)

```js
// after the map has been created and the style is loaded
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // 1️⃣  Make sure the background layer is transparent (see §3)
  const bg = map.getLayer('background');
  if (bg) {
    map.setPaintProperty('background', 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
  }

  // 2️⃣  Add the sky
  map.setSky({
    'sky-color': '#80c0ff',
    'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
    'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
    'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
    'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
    'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,
    'atmosphere-blend': 0.5
  });
});
```

> **Why call it after `style.load`?**  
> `map.setSky()` mutates the *current* style. If you call it before the style is ready, the sky will be overwritten when the style finishes loading.

---

## 3️⃣  Remove or make the background layer transparent

If your style contains a `background` layer (the default in many starter styles), it will **cover the sky** because it is rendered *below* the sky object but *above* the page background.

```js
// Option A – delete the layer completely
if (map.getLayer('background')) {
  map.removeLayer('background');
}

// Option B – keep it but make it fully transparent
if (map.getLayer('background')) {
  map.setPaintProperty('background', 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');
}
```

If you prefer to keep a subtle colour under the sky (e.g. a very light tint for night‑time maps), just set an alpha < 1.

---

## 4️⃣  Verify the camera pitch

The sky (and fog) only become visible when the map is **tilted**. You already have `pitch: 70`, which is perfect. If you ever need to change it programmatically:

```js
map.easeTo({ pitch: 70, bearing: 0, duration: 1200 });
```

---

