    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        'terrain-dem': {
          type: 'raster-dem',
          url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json',
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 12
        },
        // add your imagery source here, e.g. OSM raster tiles
        'sat': {
          type: 'raster',
          tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
          tileSize: 256
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // optional background – make it transparent
        {
          id: 'background',
          type: 'background',
          paint: { 'background-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0)' }
        },
        // imagery draped over terrain
        {
          id: 'satellite',
          type: 'raster',
          source: 'sat'
        },
        // optional hillshade (softened)
        {
          id: 'hillshade',
          type: 'hillshade',
          source: 'terrain-dem',
          paint: {
            'hillshade-method': 'multidirectional',
            'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
            'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
            'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
            'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)'
          }
        }
      ],
      terrain: { source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 }
      // note: we will add the sky dynamically below
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true   // required for 3‑D rendering
  });

  // Add the sky once the style is ready
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // make sure background is transparent (already done in the style above)
    // map.setPaintProperty('background', 'background-color', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)');

    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#80c0ff',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,
      'atmosphere-blend': 0.5
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser, tilt the map (or use the mouse wheel while holding **Shift**), and you’ll see:

* A **blue sky** that fades toward the horizon.  
* A **soft haze** (fog) that meets the terrain, giving that “ground‑meets‑sky” look.  
* The terrain remains fully 3‑D because `map.setTerrain()` is active.

---

## 6️⃣  Quick checklist (copy‑paste into your own project)

| ✅ | Action |
|---|--------|
| 1 | **Enable terrain** (`map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem', exaggeration:1})`). |
| 2 | **Add a sky object** (`map.setSky({ … })` **or** put a `sky` block in the style JSON). |
| 3 | **Make the background layer transparent** or delete it. |
| 4 | **Pitch the camera** (`pitch > 0`). |
| 5 | (Optional) **Tweak sky/fog colours** to match your visual design. |
| 6 | **Test on a low‑end device** – sky & fog are cheap, but keep the terrain source resolution reasonable. |

---

### 🎨  Common tweaks

| Property | Typical values | What it does |
|----------|----------------|--------------|
| `sky-color` | `#80c0ff` (day), `#2b3b4d` (dusk) | Overall colour of the upper sky. |
| `sky-horizon-blend` | `0.3 – 0.7` | How quickly the sky colour fades toward the horizon. |
| `horizon-color` | `#ffffff` (bright) or `#c0c0c0` (muted) | Colour of the line where sky meets terrain. |
| `fog-color` | `#c0d8ff` (light) or `#6b8eae` (misty) | Colour of the haze that blends into the ground. |
| `fog-ground-blend` | `0.4 – 0.8` | How far the fog reaches down the terrain (higher = thicker near the ground). |
| `atmosphere-blend` | `0.0 – 0.5` | Adds a subtle glow that mimics atmospheric scattering (only noticeable at high pitch). |

Play with these numbers until the sky looks natural for your map’s theme.

---

## 7️⃣  Gotchas & FAQs

| Question | Answer |
|----------|--------|
| **Do I need a `sky` layer?** | No. MapLibre only supports a **root‑level `sky` object** (or `map.setSky()`). A layer with `type: "sky"` is ignored. |
| **Why does the sky disappear after I call `map.setStyle()` again?** | `setStyle()` replaces the whole style, wiping out the sky object. Call `map.setSky()` **after** each `style.load` event, or embed the sky directly in the new style JSON. |
| **My background colour is still visible** | Check that you don’t have a **second background layer**

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** recipe for putting a pre‑rendered shaded‑relief JPEG tile set under all of your vector data (including the label layers) in a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣  What you’re really adding

* A **raster source** (`type: "raster"`).  
* A **raster layer** (`type: "raster"`).  

That’s it – the JPEG tiles are already “shaded relief”, so MapLibre does **not** need any of the elevation‑specific layer types (`hillshade`, `color‑relief`, `terrain`, …). Those are only for *DEM* sources.

---

## 2️⃣  Minimal HTML / JS skeleton

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Shaded‑relief basemap + vector data</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style you like)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // ---- your vector sources go here ----
        // e.g. "roads": { type:"vector", url:"https://.../roads.json" }
      },
      layers: [
        // ---- any background / sky / etc. you want ----
        // (the shaded‑relief raster will be inserted later)
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Wait until the style is ready
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add the raster‑DEM (shaded‑relief) source
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.addSource('shaded-relief', {
      type: 'raster',
      // 👉  Replace with your own tile URL template
      //     {z}/{x}/{y} will be substituted by the map engine
      tiles: [
        'https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
      ],
      tileSize: 256,               // most JPEG tiles are 256 px
      // optional: attribution, maxzoom, minzoom, etc.
      attribution: '© My Shaded‑Relief Tiles'
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Add the raster layer *under* the labels
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Find the first label layer in the style (or pick one you know)
    const firstLabelLayerId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol' && l.layout?.['text-field'])
                                          ?.id;

    map.addLayer(
      {
        id: 'relief-basemap',
        type: 'raster',
        source: 'shaded-relief',
        paint: {
          // You can tone the raster down if you like:
          // 'raster-opacity': 0.9,
          // 'raster-brightness-min': 0.6,
          // 'raster-contrast': 1.2
        }
      },
      // Insert *before* the first label layer so everything else stays on top.
      // If you have no label layer, omit the second argument and the raster will be
      // added on top of the existing layers (you can then move it later).
      firstLabelLayerId
    );

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  (Optional) Add your vector layers / labels
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Example: a simple line layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-roads',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'roads',          // <-- your vector source id
      'source-layer': 'roads', // <-- the layer name inside the vector tileset
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#ff6600',
        'line-width': 2
      }
    });

    // Example: a label layer (will automatically sit above the raster)
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'city-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'places',
      'source-layer': 'places',
      layout: {
        'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
        'text-size': 12,
        'text-anchor': 'top'
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#000',
        'text-halo-color': '#fff',
        'text-halo-width': 1
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **1** | Starts a MapLibre map with any base style you like (you can even start from an empty style). |
| **2** | Waits for the style to be fully loaded before mutating it. |
| **3** | Registers a **raster source** that points at your JPEG tiles. `tiles` is an array of URL templates; you can also add `minzoom`, `maxzoom`, `attribution`, etc. |
| **4** | Adds a **raster layer** that draws the source. The second argument to `addLayer` (`firstLabelLayerId`) tells MapLibre to insert the raster *just before* the first label layer, guaranteeing that all your vector data (including symbols/labels) stays on top. If you don’t know a label layer id, you can call `map.moveLayer('relief-basemap', 'some-other-layer')` later. |
| **5** | Shows where you would add your own vector layers. They automatically appear **above** the raster because they are added *after* the raster layer. |

---

## 3️⃣  Quick checklist – “Did I forget something?”

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| **Source type** – `type: "raster"` (not `"raster-dem"`). |
| **Tile URL** – ends in `.jpg` / `.jpeg` (or `.png`). |
| **Layer order** – raster layer inserted *before* any `symbol` (label) layers. |
| **Opacity / tone** – optional `paint` properties (`raster-opacity`, `raster-brightness-min`, `raster-contrast`, etc.) if the basemap is too dark or too bright. |
| **No elevation props** – don’t try `hillshade-*`, `color-relief-*`, or `setTerrain()` on a JPEG basemap; those only work with DEM (`raster-dem`) sources. |
| **Attribution** – add it to the source (or a separate `background` layer) to stay compliant with the tile provider’s license. |
| **CORS** – make sure the tile server sends `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin:*` (or your domain) so the browser can fetch the tiles. |

---

## 4️⃣  Common “gotchas” & how to fix them

| Symptom | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **The raster never appears** | Source URL is wrong, CORS blocked, or the layer is added *after* a `background` layer that covers the whole canvas. | Open the Network tab → verify tile requests succeed (200). If you see CORS errors, enable CORS on the tile server. Ensure the raster layer is added **before** any `background` layer or move it with `map.moveLayer`. |
| **Labels are hidden behind the raster** | The raster layer was added *after* the label (`symbol`) layers, so it sits on top. | Insert the raster **before** the first label layer (`addLayer(..., firstLabelLayerId)`) or later call `map.moveLayer('relief-basemap', 'some-label-layer-id')`. |
| **The basemap looks too dark / too bright** | JPEG tiles may have been rendered for a different background. | Use raster paint properties, e.g. `{'raster-brightness-min': 0.6, 'raster-contrast': 1.2}`. |
| **Tiles are blurry at high zoom** | The source’s `maxzoom` is lower than the map’s current zoom, so MapLibre falls back to lower‑resolution tiles. | Set `maxzoom` on the source to the highest zoom your tiles support, or limit the map’s `maxZoom`. |
| **You want a sky/fog effect** | That only works with true 3‑D terrain (`raster-dem`) and `map.setSky()`. | Not applicable for a pre‑rendered JPEG basemap – you’d need a DEM if you want a sky/fog. |

---

## 5️⃣  TL;DR – One‑liner summary

```js
map.addSource('relief', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
  tileSize: 256,
  attribution: '© My Shaded‑Relief Tiles'
});

const firstLabel = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol' && l.layout?.['text-field'])?.id;
map.addLayer({ id: 'relief-basemap', type: 'raster', source: 'relief' }, firstLabel);
```

That’s all you need to get a beautiful pre‑rendered shaded‑relief JPEG basemap **under** your vector data with the labels staying on top. Happy mapping! 🚀
